\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Calculated the exact order of finite imaginary-time moments in a gapless spectral model, identifying a failure of a naive all-orders argument.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the full supplied definition, catalogue wording, source qualifications and listed references for 30005662. No fresh status or novelty search was performed. The argument below proves its auxiliary statements directly and does not import an unverified external theorem.

\paragraph{Testing imaginary-time moment integrability.} Consider a nonnegative spectral measure \(d\nu(E)=E^\alpha\mathbf1_{(0,1)}(E)\,dE\), with \(\alpha>-1\), and its correlation \(C(t)=\int_0^1 e^{-tE}E^\alpha dE\), \(t\geq0\). This is realized on \(L^2((0,1),E^\alpha dE)\) by multiplication by \(E\) and the vector \(1\). The spectrum touches zero. For an integer \(j\geq0\), nonnegativity permits exchanging integrals, and repeated integration by parts in the inner integral gives
\[
 \int_0^\infty t^j C(t)\,dt
 =j!\int_0^1 E^{\alpha-j-1}\,dE
 =\frac{j!}{\alpha-j}\quad\hbox{if }\alpha>j.
\]
For \(\alpha\leq j\) it diverges; equality is a logarithmic divergence. Thus the zeroth time moment can exist while the first fails, for example \(0<\alpha\leq1\).
\paragraph{Attempted extension and obstruction.} Any source expansion coefficient that requires this absolute \(j\)-th moment cannot be justified from decay sufficient only for lower moments. The example supplies a gapless correlation with an exactly computable order cutoff. It is not a no-go theorem for adiabatic evolution: cancellations, a different observable or a resummation may avoid that moment requirement.
\paragraph{Full-source gap.} To obtain the requested expansion one must derive appropriate low-energy spectral weights for specified noninteracting or interacting observables, control higher connected correlations, and bound the remainder on the chosen slow time scale. The example has no driven Hamiltonian and no proved dynamical error bound. Its role is to expose the precise loss of uniform moment estimates when a gapped proof is copied to a gapless system.

